;; Where M-futon-seams actually stands against futon4/holes/mission-lifecycle.md.
;;
;; Status per phase is read from that phase's EXIT CRITERION against the
;; evidence, not from the mission's Status line. Where the two disagree the row
;; says so, and the disagreement is the point: this afternoon's work produced
;; artefacts belonging to MAP, DERIVE, ARGUE and INSTANTIATE while the mission
;; file still says IDENTIFY and has no section for any of them.
;;
;; Joe assigned the mission to claude-1 on 2026-09-24 ("claude-1 owns it,
;; we're collaborating on the AIF + PROOF-2 interpretation"), so the Status
;; line and the phase sections have been advanced to what the evidence
;; supports -- and the out-of-order work is stated in the mission rather than
;; smoothed away. Phases 2-7 are now written in the mission itself, so every
;; row below anchors to a real span and none is a placeholder.
;;
;; :status is :not-started, :in-progress, :blocked or :exit-met. :blocked
;; carries :blocked-on and means the phase's own work is done and its exit
;; waits on another phase -- the lifecycle's word for it, applied to a phase.
;; NOTHING USES IT. ARGUE briefly did, on an invented dependency on DOCUMENT
;; that I read into its exit criterion; Joe caught it. The phases run in
;; order and a later phase does not gate an earlier one. Kept because the
;; state is real, but a phase claiming it should be doubted first.
;;
;; :verdict-source says WHERE a phase's verdict is written. :prose means the
;; section carries an exact **Met.** / **Not met.** / **Not started.** line and
;; scripts/verdict_check.py holds it to agree with :status here. :data-only
;; means the phase has no exit-criterion paragraph and no verdict line, so its
;; closure is recorded here and nowhere else -- HEAD and IDENTIFY, which
;; predate the phase write-ups and carry the operator's voice.
;;
;; This was a hardcoded name list inside verdict_check.py until 2026-09-24,
;; which is this mission's own defect in its own tooling: a property of a
;; phase, living in a script instead of being declared. A reader that sees
;; only prose, or only data, misreads the mission; now either can discover
;; which phases it cannot see.
;;
;; :mission-anchor is an exact span of the mission at the recorded sha, as the
;; annotations are. A phase with none gets a PLACEHOLDER in the page -- a
;; heading that says the mission has not written this phase yet, drawn so it
;; cannot be mistaken for mission text.
;; Authored by claude-1, 2026-09-24. DRAFT pending claude-10's review.
